  "worked_solution": "Given a normal random variable $X\u223cN(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(X<x)=P(Z<\\frac{x-\\mu }{\\sigma })\n$$\n\nwhere $Z\u223cN(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu =0$ and $\\sigma =10.$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<-11.7) & =P(Z<\\frac{-11.7-0}{10}) \\\\  & =P(Z<-1.17) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le -1.17) \\\\  & =\u03a6(-1.17)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070250/assets/9b47010de67e748c75465b0ad1ec54d984ec7eaac6721ae9fb9b11cb60c16b16.png)\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\n\u03a6(-1.17)=0.1210.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(X<-11.7)=0.1210.\n$$",
